WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.891
Anne stayed up all night in her room, thinking about the fact that Gilbert was dying.

2
00:00:06.892 --> 00:00:11.271
The rain beat down and the wind roared terribly outside.

3
00:00:11.272 --> 00:00:18.992
With the possibility of Gilbert gone forever, she knew now that she loved him—she had always loved him!

4
00:00:18.993 --> 00:00:25.777
If she had not been so blind . . . so foolish . . . she could be nursing him now.

5
00:00:25.778 --> 00:00:32.706
Anne sobbed. Gilbert would never know how she felt; he would die thinking that she did not care.

6
00:00:32.707 --> 00:00:39.233
Anne ached at the thought of life without Gilbert—the black years of emptiness stretching before her!

7
00:00:39.234 --> 00:00:47.370
She cowered down by her window and wished, for the first time in her happy young life, that she could die too.

8
00:00:47.371 --> 00:00:52.280
Early the next morning, she drove the buggy as fast as she could to the Blythe house.

9
00:00:52.281 --> 00:00:56.230
On the way she met one of the men who worked for Mr. Blythe.

10
00:00:56.231 --> 00:01:00.794
"Do you have any news of Gilbert Blythe?" she asked breathlessly.

11
00:01:00.795 --> 00:01:12.316
"Yes, I do," the man said. "He suddenly got much better late last night, and the doctor said he'll recover. I think he'll be all right."

12
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:17.043
Anne turned the buggy around and drove slowly back to Green Gables.

13
00:01:17.044 --> 00:01:23.907
She could not think straight. Her head was buzzing, and she felt more alive than she had in a long time.

14
00:01:23.908 --> 00:01:30.531
This was love! For Anne it was the most beautiful morning she had ever seen.

15
00:01:30.532 --> 00:01:37.920
The birds were singing, the sun was shining brightly, and best of all, Gilbert was alive.

16
00:01:37.921 --> 00:01:42.291
After his recovery Gilbert visited Green Gables quite often.

17
00:01:42.292 --> 00:01:45.824
Anne did not mention her change in feelings to him.

18
00:01:45.825 --> 00:01:50.007
She believed that Gilbert and Christine Stuart were still a couple.

19
00:01:50.008 --> 00:01:53.172
So Anne tried her best to be just friends.

20
00:01:53.173 --> 00:01:59.230
It was difficult, though, because Anne no longer wanted friendship; she wanted Gilbert's love.

21
00:01:59.231 --> 00:02:05.213
Anne was haunted by a miserable fear that her mistake could never be fixed.

22
00:02:05.214 --> 00:02:10.359
When Gilbert came to Green Gables one afternoon, Anne was waiting for him.

23
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.891
They walked to the graveyard together to put flowers on Matthew's grave.

24
00:02:14.892 --> 00:02:21.820
It was spring again, and the graveyard was particularly beautiful with all the plum trees in bloom.

25
00:02:21.821 --> 00:02:27.552
Anne gently put the flowers on Matthew's grave, and then sat under a tree with Gilbert.

26
00:02:27.553 --> 00:02:34.098
"This place is so beautiful," said Anne softly. "It's like a dream."

27
00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:37.369
"Do you have any dreams, Anne?" asked Gilbert.

28
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:45.560
The tone of his voice sounded different, the way it had sounded that miserable evening in the orchard at Patty's Place over a year ago.

29
00:02:45.561 --> 00:02:51.766
Anne's heart beat wildly. Despite trembling inside she gave a light answer.

30
00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:56.682
"Of course, I have dreams!" she said. "Everybody has.

31
00:02:56.683 --> 00:03:01.595
We would be as good as dead if we had nothing left to dream about!"

32
00:03:01.596 --> 00:03:06.730
Gilbert turned to look at Anne. "I have a dream," he said.

33
00:03:06.731 --> 00:03:11.828
"I persist in dreaming it, although it has often seemed to me that it could never come

34
00:03:11.829 --> 00:03:21.421
true. I dream of a nice home with a fireplace in it, a cat and a dog, a big garden with fruits and vegetables, good friends coming to visit . . . and

35
00:03:21.422 --> 00:03:26.515
you! You are my dream, Anne. You always have

36
00:03:26.516 --> 00:03:30.495
been." Anne wanted to speak, but she could find no

37
00:03:30.496 --> 00:03:35.862
words. Happiness was breaking over her like a wave. It almost frightened

38
00:03:35.863 --> 00:03:39.886
her. "I asked you a question over two years ago, Anne," Gilbert

39
00:03:39.887 --> 00:03:43.626
said. "If I ask it again today, will you give me a different

40
00:03:43.627 --> 00:03:50.263
answer?" Anne still could not speak, but she lifted her head and looked steadily into Gilbert's

41
00:03:50.264 --> 00:03:57.523
eyes. She could look nowhere else. Her eyes shone with love, and Gilbert needed no other

42
00:03:57.524 --> 00:04:01.694
answer. Gilbert dared to smile, and then Anne smiled

43
00:04:01.695 --> 00:04:05.730
too. She was almost afraid to believe that what was happening was

44
00:04:05.731 --> 00:04:09.035
true. "Surely, this is a dream," she

45
00:04:09.036 --> 00:04:20.264
thought. "Gilbert, my love, my true love, telling me he loves me, on a beautiful afternoon in Avonlea . . .

46
00:04:20.265 --> 00:04:28.098
" "I thought you loved Christine Stuart," Anne later told him reproachfully, as if she had never gone out with Roy

47
00:04:28.099 --> 00:04:32.954
Gardner. Gilbert laughed. "Christine was engaged to somebody in her

48
00:04:32.955 --> 00:04:37.273
hometown. I knew it, and she knew that I knew it. I was friends with her

49
00:04:37.274 --> 00:04:43.841
brother. He told me his sister was coming to Halifax, and asked me if I would look after her. So I

50
00:04:43.842 --> 00:04:48.426
did." Gilbert continued, "Christine is one of the nicest girls I've ever

51
00:04:48.427 --> 00:04:52.215
known. I knew college gossip said we were in love, but I didn't

52
00:04:52.216 --> 00:04:57.008
care. After you told me you could never love me, Anne, nothing mattered much to me for a

53
00:04:57.009 --> 00:05:01.975
while. There was nobody else. There never could be anybody but you for

54
00:05:01.976 --> 00:05:06.574
me. I've loved you ever since that day you broke your slate over my head in

55
00:05:06.575 --> 00:05:12.422
school." "I don't see how you could keep on loving me when I was such a little fool," said

56
00:05:12.423 --> 00:05:22.127
Anne. "Well, I tried to stop," said Gilbert honestly, "and it wasn't easy being told every week by some busybody that you were almost

57
00:05:22.128 --> 00:05:25.018
engaged! Things only changed a few weeks

58
00:05:25.019 --> 00:05:30.562
ago. I had been really sick—close to dying, the doctor said—when a letter arrived from Phil Gordon.

59
00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:34.751
She told me that there was nothing between you and Roy, and that I should try again.

60
00:05:34.752 --> 00:05:41.425
Well, the doctor was amazed at my rapid recovery after that." Gilbert smiled lovingly at Anne.

61
00:05:41.426 --> 00:05:48.878
Anne laughed and then shivered. "I can never forget the night I thought you were dying, Gilbert.

62
00:05:48.879 --> 00:05:56.451
Oh, I knew . . . I knew then how much I loved you . . . and I feared it was too late."

63
00:05:56.452 --> 00:06:02.799
"But it wasn't, Anne, my love," Gilbert said, and he drew Anne close to him and kissed her.

64
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:14.307
They walked home together in the dusk, happy and together in love.

